Q: I need to make Java Swing Timer more accurate A: <p>Your variable naming is terrible, making your code hard to follow.  Primary example: speeds express progress per unit time, but what your code labels as &quot;speeds&quot; have units of time per progress.</p>
<p>It seems like that may have confused even you, because it means that your <code>fillFactor</code> has units of time/progress<sup>2</sup>, with mixed specific progress units, which isn't very useful.  What's more, as used, your <code>thisAnimX</code> (very hard to decipher) has units of length (progress), so it's nonsensical to add that to <code>fillFactor</code> as the units don't match.</p>
<p>As a time per progress, your <code>thisSpeed</code> actually is potentially appropriate as the first argument to the <code>javax.swing.Timer</code> constructor (a delay), even though its name suggests otherwise. However, it's not immediately evident from your code whether the value you've computed actually makes sense or not, and it hurts my brain to try to check that.</p>
<p>Let's start there to sort this out.  It is fairly conventional to choose a fixed target tick size (or constant target frame rate, which is equivalent).  Suppose we go with a constant 16 millisecond tick, for a frame rate of a little more than 60 Hz:</p>
<pre><code>final static int TICK_SIZE_MS = 16;
</code></pre>
<p>It's then straightforward to combine that with your tempo to compute the number of ticks per beat:</p>
<pre><code>//                 (ms / min)    (ms / tick)   (beats / min)
int ticksPerBeat =      60000 / TICK_SIZE_MS /   wantedTempo;    // truncated
</code></pre>
<p>And from there, to the number of ticks for the current syllable and note, supposing one quarter note per beat:</p>
<pre><code>int ticksForThisNote;

// ...

int quarterBeatsForThisNote = switch (noteLength) {
    case &quot;s&quot; -&gt; 1;
    case &quot;e&quot; -&gt; 2;
    case &quot;q&quot; -&gt; 4;
    case &quot;h&quot; -&gt; 8;
    case &quot;w&quot; -&gt; 16;
    default  -&gt; 0;
};

ticksForThisNote = (ticksPerBeat * quarterBeatsForThisNote + 3) / 4;    // rounded up
</code></pre>
<p>Rather than a width increment, I'd probably use a straight tick counter (replacing <code>thisAnimX</code>):</p>
<pre><code>int tickCount = 0;
</code></pre>
<p>Then, each tick, you increment the tick count and compute the animation progress width from that:</p>
<pre><code>        // this timer is used animate the label fill:
        thisTimer = new Timer(TICK_SIZE_MS, e -&gt; {
            tickCount += 1;
            ((KaraokeLyric) thisLabel).setAnimX(thisWidth * tickCount / ticksForThisNote);
            thisLabel.repaint();
            // once the label is full, the current timer stops and the next one starts
            if (tickCount &gt;= ticksForThisNote) {
                ((Timer) e.getSource()).stop();
                tickCount = 0;
                SingAlong.startNextAnim();
            } 
        });
</code></pre>
<p>I don't know about you, but that organization and naming is much easier for me to follow than yours. I can tell at a glance that each step in the computation is right, and that the results are used in a way that is consistent with each variable's significance.</p>
<p>Additional notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>With a constant tick size like that, you should strongly consider using a single <code>Timer</code> that just keeps running, as the music certainly will do. That probably wouldn't belong to any <code>NoteSyllable</code> instance. Rather, you would want some controller component to receive the ticks and dispatch them to the current <code>NoteSyllable</code> and lyric.</p>
</li>
<li><p>That you keep casting <code>NoteSyllable.thisLabel</code> is a clear sign that you have declared too general a type for it.  You have a hard dependency on it being a <code>KaraokeLyric</code>, so declare it with that type.  That makes the constraint clear and removes the need for casting.</p>
</li>
</ul>
